<!DOCTYPE html><html lang=3D"en"><head><meta http-equiv=3D"Content-Type" co=
ntent=3D"text/html; charset=3DUTF-8"><link rel=3D"stylesheet" type=3D"text/=
css" href=3D"cid:css-2e8fa9b2-9aa3-4828-9850-7b636c421cfc@mhtml.blink" />

<meta name=3D"viewport" content=3D"width=3Ddevice-width, initial-scale=3D1.=
0">
<title>Educosys Claude - Architecture Notes</title>

</head>
<body>
<div class=3D"page">

  <h1>Educosys Claude - Architecture &amp; Design Notes</h1>

  <!-- =E2=94=80=E2=94=80 LAYERED ARCHITECTURE =E2=94=80=E2=94=80 -->
  <h2>Layered Architecture</h2>
  <div class=3D"section">
    <p>The codebase is split into discrete layers. Each layer has one job. =
No layer reaches across to another layer's internals.</p>
    <div style=3D"margin: 1rem 0;">

      <div class=3D"layer">
        <span class=3D"layer-tag">CLI / REPL</span>
        <span class=3D"layer-desc">The entry point. Responsible for talking=
 to the user - receiving input, displaying output, managing commands. Nothi=
ng below this layer knows about the user.</span>
      </div>
      <div class=3D"layer-arrow">=E2=86=93</div>

      <div class=3D"layer">
        <span class=3D"layer-tag">Agent</span>
        <span class=3D"layer-desc">Responsible for reasoning and decision-m=
aking. Takes a question, decides what to do, calls tools if needed, returns=
 an answer. The "brain" of the system.</span>
      </div>
      <div class=3D"layer-arrow">=E2=86=93</div>

      <div class=3D"layer">
        <span class=3D"layer-tag">Tools</span>
        <span class=3D"layer-desc">Responsible for giving the agent hands. =
Each tool is one capability - search the codebase, read a file, run a comma=
nd. The agent decides when to use them.</span>
      </div>
      <div class=3D"layer-arrow">=E2=86=93</div>

      <div class=3D"layer">
        <span class=3D"layer-tag">Skills</span>
        <span class=3D"layer-desc">Responsible for higher-level composed ca=
pabilities. A skill combines multiple tools and agent calls into one reusab=
le workflow - e.g. "explain this PR" or "review this file". Skills are what=
 end-users think of as features.</span>
      </div>
      <div class=3D"layer-arrow">=E2=86=93</div>

      <div class=3D"layer">
        <span class=3D"layer-tag">MCP</span>
        <span class=3D"layer-desc">Responsible for external integrations. M=
CP servers expose third-party systems (GitHub, Jira, Slack) as tools the ag=
ent can call. Keeps integration logic separate from core agent logic.</span=
>
      </div>
      <div class=3D"layer-arrow">=E2=86=93</div>

      <div class=3D"layer">
        <span class=3D"layer-tag">Context / RAG</span>
        <span class=3D"layer-desc">Responsible for knowledge retrieval. Kno=
ws how to index a codebase and find relevant pieces of it given a query. Co=
mpletely unaware of the agent or tools above it.</span>
      </div>
      <div class=3D"layer-arrow">=E2=86=93</div>

      <div class=3D"layer">
        <span class=3D"layer-tag">Memory</span>
        <span class=3D"layer-desc">Responsible for remembering. Short-term =
keeps the current conversation. Long-term keeps facts, summaries, and behav=
ioral patterns across sessions.</span>
      </div>
      <div class=3D"layer-arrow">=E2=86=93</div>

      <div class=3D"layer">
        <span class=3D"layer-tag">Safety</span>
        <span class=3D"layer-desc">Responsible for guardrails. Validates in=
puts before they reach the agent and outputs before they reach the user. Bl=
ocks harmful content, enforces scope, prevents prompt injection. Nothing le=
aves the system without passing through here.</span>
      </div>
      <div class=3D"layer-arrow">=E2=86=93</div>

      <div class=3D"layer">
        <span class=3D"layer-tag">Freshness</span>
        <span class=3D"layer-desc">Responsible for keeping the index up to =
date. Detects when the codebase has changed and triggers re-indexing. Witho=
ut this, the RAG layer answers questions about stale code.</span>
      </div>
      <div class=3D"layer-arrow">=E2=86=93</div>

      <div class=3D"layer">
        <span class=3D"layer-tag">LLM</span>
        <span class=3D"layer-desc">Responsible for model access. Decides wh=
ich LLM and embedder to instantiate. Everything above this layer is model-a=
gnostic.</span>
      </div>
      <div class=3D"layer-arrow">=E2=86=93</div>

      <div class=3D"layer">
        <span class=3D"layer-tag">Config</span>
        <span class=3D"layer-desc">Responsible for application-wide setting=
s. A single source of truth for how the system is configured - providers, m=
odes, limits. Every layer reads from it, nothing writes to it at runtime.</=
span>
      </div>
      <div class=3D"layer-arrow">=E2=86=93</div>

      <div class=3D"layer">
        <span class=3D"layer-tag">Observability</span>
        <span class=3D"layer-desc">Responsible for visibility. Structured l=
ogging so you can see exactly what the system is doing at runtime - which l=
ayer, which operation, which outcome.</span>
      </div>
      <div class=3D"layer-arrow">=E2=86=93</div>

      <div class=3D"layer">
        <span class=3D"layer-tag">Eval</span>
        <span class=3D"layer-desc">Responsible for measuring quality. Share=
d golden datasets test every layer independently - retrieval (Recall@k, MRR=
), context (precision, recall), generation (faithfulness, relevancy via RAG=
AS), and agent (task success rate, step accuracy).</span>
      </div>

    </div>
  </div>

  <hr>

  <!-- =E2=94=80=E2=94=80 PACKAGE & DEV TOOLING =E2=94=80=E2=94=80 -->
  <h2>Package &amp; Dev Tooling</h2>
  <div class=3D"section">
    <h3>Poetry vs pipx</h3>
    <p>We use <code>poetry install</code> for development - it installs the=
 package in editable mode so code changes reflect immediately without reins=
tall. <code>pipx</code> is for distributing a finished CLI tool to end-user=
s.</p>
    <div class=3D"decision">
      <div class=3D"decision-label">Decision</div>
      <div class=3D"decision-text">Use <code>poetry run educosys_claude</co=
de> during development. Use <code>pipx</code> to test the installed package=
 from another directory.</div>
    </div>

    <h3>config.yaml vs .env - what goes where and why</h3>
    <p><code>config.yaml</code> and <code>.env</code> serve completely diff=
erent purposes and should never be mixed.</p>
    <div class=3D"decision">
      <div class=3D"decision-label">config.yaml - behaviour config</div>
      <div class=3D"decision-text">Controls how the app behaves: which LLM =
provider, which vector store, model names, chunk sizes, collection names, m=
emory limits. Safe to commit to git. Changes here switch features, not cred=
entials.</div>
    </div>
    <div class=3D"decision">
      <div class=3D"decision-label">.env - secrets</div>
      <div class=3D"decision-text">API keys, passwords, URLs that change pe=
r environment (dev vs prod). Never committed to git - always in <code>.giti=
gnore</code>. Read via <code>os.getenv()</code> at runtime, not loaded into=
 the config dict.</div>
    </div>
    <div class=3D"alt">
      <div class=3D"alt-label">Why separate?</div>
      <div class=3D"alt-text">A teammate can clone the repo, see exactly ho=
w the app is configured in <code>config.yaml</code>, and supply their own k=
eys in <code>.env</code>. If secrets lived in <code>config.yaml</code>, one=
 accidental commit leaks every key. If behaviour config lived in <code>.env=
</code>, there's no visible record of what settings the app runs with.</div=
>
    </div>
  </div>

  <hr>
</div></body></html>
